From 79f1442e76325013b8ae6f95f03cafeddc380fb5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: piratenpanda <benjamin@lebsanft.org>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 04:08:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tests: unit and pack tests for the spektrafilm simulation
 engine

Ports the tests the reference python implementation runs against the same
algorithms into two cmocka suites, so a change to the engine that breaks one of
them fails the build rather than quietly changing everybody's renders. Each
test names the upstream test it came from, so it stays clear which side moved
when the two disagree.

test_spektra_sim.c needs no data. It covers the reinhard knee and both gamut
compressors, the cdfs density curve model, the s023 chemistry morph including
the developer-exhaustion solver that has to hold midgray still, and the DIR
coupler correction -- from tests/test_gamut_compression.py, test_parametric.py,
test_morph_curves.py and test_couplers.py. The per-pixel entry points among
those are fed from a hand-built sf_sim_t carrying a linear density ramp, so the
expected values are closed forms the test states itself rather than borrowing
the interpolator it is checking.

test_spektra_pack.c is the half that needs real data, from
tests/test_profiles.py, test_enlarger_filters.py and test_pipeline_smoke.py: it
loads the pack darktable ships, checks the profiles and the neutral filter
database, and runs the assembled per-pixel chain -- valid output for ordinary
and extreme input, a flat patch staying flat and bit-identical across runs,
brightness and exposure compensation ordered the way their signs say, scan-film
mode building with no paper, and the quality LUT tracking the exact spectral
path. CMake hands it the pack directory, resolving either a directory holding
pack.json or one holding a single versioned subdirectory, so the suite does not
have to be taught the current spektrafilm release. With no pack on disk every
test in it skips: a machine that cannot answer the question should not report
the engine as broken, and a build that goes red for the wrong reason is one
people learn to ignore.

Its tolerances stay loose. What is pinned down is that the pipeline stays
finite, bounded, ordered and reproducible on real data; exact values belong in
a regression baseline against a pinned pack, where a deliberate change to the
model can be re-blessed in one place instead of scattering magic numbers
through the suite. Where a measurement said otherwise the bound follows it: the
33-step quality LUT lands within 6e-4 of exact spectral on the 0.3.3 pack, so
that check allows 5e-3 rather than an invented number.

Profiles are read as carrying gaps, not as fully populated: a stock is NaN
(null in the JSON, upstream's _json_safe) wherever it was never measured, which
for Portra 400 is both ends of the base density and a fifth of the channel
densities. The assertions are that nothing is infinite -- a parse or arithmetic
fault rather than a gap -- and that real samples are present.

Comparisons go through a local assert_double_close() rather than cmocka's
assert_float_equal(), which casts both operands and the epsilon to float. From
cmocka 1.1.2 on that macro shadows the fallback in tests/unittests/util/assert.h,
and an epsilon of 1e-9 then sits below the float resolution of values around 1,
so the tolerances here would silently stop meaning anything. assert_double_equal()
would be the right tool but 1.1.0, the minimum the build accepts, has neither
macro, so the suites carry their own and work on both.

Two of the ports could not be literal. Upstream's knee tests assume its own
threshold of 0.815 while spektrafilm ships zero, so the knee is tested through
its parameters instead of only at the shipped ones, and the chromaticity test
that upstream states as exact identity below the threshold becomes identity to
within rounding. And upstream asserts the coupler matrix is diagonal without
interlayer inhibition; that matrix is built inside sf_sim_build() from a loaded
profile, so the property is asserted one step further down, on the correction
itself, which is where it matters anyway.
